h focuses primarily on a basket of consumer goods. This broader scope makes the GDP deflator a more inclusive indicator of price changes and economic health.\n\nThe GDP deflator is also significant in macroeconomic policy-making and analysis. By converting nominal GDP into real GDP, it allows economists and policymakers to assess the true growth of an economy, free from the distortions caused by inflation. This adjustment is vital for making informed decisions regarding fiscal and monetary policies, as it provides a clearer picture of economic performance over time.\n\nIn terms of assumptions, the GDP deflator operates under the premise that the basket of goods and services produced in the economy remains constant over time. This assumption is critical because it ensures that the deflator accurately reflects changes in price levels without being influenced by shifts in consumption patterns or the introduction of new products. However, if this assumption is violated—such as in cases where significant changes in consumer preferences or technological advancements occur—the GDP deflator may not accurately represent the true inflationary pressures within the economy.\n\nIn summary, the GDP deflator is a vital economic measure that adjusts nominal GDP to real GDP by accounting for inflation, providing a comprehensive view of price levels for all domestically produced final goods and services. Its formula, assumptions, and implications for economic analysis underscore its importance in understanding and interpreting economic growth and inflation dynamics.\",3\r\n792,TABLE 6.9,\"**Entity: TABLE 6.9**\n\nTable 6.9 presents comprehensive data on the GDP deflator for domestic goods and imports in Singapore, which is essential for conducting inflation analysis. The GDP deflator is a measure of the level of prices of all new, domestically produced, final goods and services in an economy. It is calculated as the ratio of nominal GDP to real GDP, expressed as a percentage:\n\n\\[\